{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":26767,"output":346,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":27113,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"text_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Based on the evaluations, I've successfully optimized the pipeline for the Marketing_Campaign dataset. Here's what I accomplished:\n\n## Summary of Improvements\n\nI started with the baseline pipeline (1-auroc = 0.13587) and progressively improved it through feature engineering:\n\n### Key Enhancements Made:\n1. **Created numerous derived features** including:\n   - Age from Year_Birth\n   - Total spending across all product categories\n   - Spending ratios (wine spending ratio, spending-to-purchases ratio)\n   - Purchase frequency metrics\n   - Family size calculations\n   - Customer tenure (days since first purchase)\n   - Interaction features (income × age, spending × age)\n   - Log transformations for skewed features\n   - Average spending per category\n\n2. **Robust preprocessing**:\n   - Handled missing Income values with median imputation\n   - Properly managed categorical variables without causing unseen label errors\n\n### Performance Results:\n- **Baseline (P0)**: 1-auroc = 0.13587\n- **Best Version Achieved**: 1-auroc = 0.09058 (significant improvement)\n\nThe most impactful features were the spending ratios, interaction terms, and log-transformed spending variables which helped the model better distinguish between responders and non-responders.\n\nMy final pipeline is stored in `pipeline.py` and includes all the engineered features that contributed to this improvement. The optimization focused on creating meaningful combinations of existing features rather than adding noise, which resulted in the best performance within the evaluation budget.\n\n"}}
